{"name":"Peter Pan","hex":"#19a700","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Peter Pan","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A man who acts like a child and does not accept the responsibilities of adulthood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Peter_Pan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peter Pan (character)","blocks":[{"text":"Peter Pan is a fictional character created by Scottish novelist and playwright J. M. Barrie. A free-spirited and mischievous young boy who can fly and never grows up, he spends his never-ending childhood having adventures on the mythical island of Neverland as the leader of the Lost Boys, interacting with fairies, pirates, mermaids, Native Americans, and occasionally ordinary children from the world outside Neverland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Peter_pan_1911_pipes_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Peter_pan_1911_pipes_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Pan_(character)","wikidata":"Q107190"},{"title":"Peter Pan (play and novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Peter Pan; or, The Boy Who Wouldn't Grow Up, often known simply as Peter Pan, is a work by British novelist J. M. Barrie, in the form of a 1904 play and a 1911 novel titled Peter and Wendy. Both versions tell the story of Peter Pan, a mischievous little boy who can fly, and has many adventures on the island of Neverland that is inhabited by mermaids, fairies, Native Americans and pirates. The Peter Pan stories also involve the characters Wendy Darling and her two younger brothers John and Michael, Peter's fairy Tinker Bell, the Lost Boys, and the pirate Captain Hook. The play and novel were inspired by Barrie's friendship with the Llewelyn Davies family."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Peter-pan-play-announcement.jpg/330px-Peter-pan-play-announcement.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Pan_(play_and_novel)","wikidata":"Q3435337"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Peter_Pan%2C_by_Oliver_Herford%2C_1907.png/330px-Peter_Pan%2C_by_Oliver_Herford%2C_1907.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Peter_Pan%2C_by_Oliver_Herford%2C_1907.png/330px-Peter_Pan%2C_by_Oliver_Herford%2C_1907.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1907 illustration of Peter Pan by Oliver Herford ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeter_Pan%2C_by_Oliver_Herford%2C_1907.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Peter_Pan_1915_cover.jpg/250px-Peter_Pan_1915_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Peter_Pan_1915_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Peter_Pan_1915_cover.jpg/250px-Peter_Pan_1915_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cover of 1915 edition of J. M. Barrie's novel, first published in 1911, illustrated by F. D. Bedford[21]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeter_Pan_1915_cover.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Peter_Pan_disney.jpg/500px-Peter_Pan_disney.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Peter_Pan_disney.jpg/500px-Peter_Pan_disney.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Peter Pan, as he appears in Walt Disney's film adaptation (1953)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeter_Pan_disney.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikime